  <!-- 05 | ROBOTIC MACHINING -->

  <section class="portfolio-system">

    <div class="portfolio-section-header">

      <span>
        05 | Robotic machining
      </span>

      <h2>
        A flexible machine tool with different constraints.
      </h2>

    </div>


    <div class="system-grid">

      <figure class="system-overview">

        <img
          src="/images/projects/robot-milling/system-overview.jpg"
          alt="Overall robotic machining architecture"
        />

        <figcaption>
          System architecture | Robot, spindle, workpiece and toolpath
        </figcaption>

      </figure>


      <div class="subsystem-stack">

        <figure>

          <img
            src="/images/projects/robot-milling/tool-detail.jpg"
            alt="Robotic milling spindle and cutting tool"
          />

          <figcaption>
            End effector | Spindle and cutting tool
          </figcaption>

        </figure>


        <div class="subsystem-copy">

          <h3>
            Mechanical integration
          </h3>

          <p>
            Adding a milling spindle transforms the robot wrist into a machining
            end effector.
          </p>

          <p>
            The resulting system must account for spindle mass, tool orientation,
            robot reach, structural flexibility and machining forces throughout
            the trajectory.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 06 | CUTTING CONDITIONS -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      06
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Cutting conditions
      </h2>

      <p>
        Robotic machining requires particular attention to cutting conditions.
      </p>

      <p>
        Unlike a conventional machine tool, an industrial robot generally has a
        more compliant structure, meaning that machining forces can have a greater
        influence on positioning accuracy and vibration.
      </p>

      <p>
        Cutting parameters therefore had to be selected while considering both
        material removal requirements and the mechanical limits of the robotic
        system.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Cutting speed
        </li>

        <li>
          Feed rate
        </li>

        <li>
          Depth of cut
        </li>

        <li>
          Tool engagement
        </li>

        <li>
          Robot configuration
        </li>
      </ul>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 07 | BEZIER SURFACES -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      07
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Bézier surfaces
      </h2>

      <p>
        The project also involved machining geometrically complex surfaces
        described using Bézier representations.
      </p>

      <p>
        Bézier surfaces provide a continuous mathematical description of a shape
        based on control points, making them useful for representing smooth
        free-form geometry.
      </p>

      <p>
        This required linking geometric modelling with the generation of machining
        trajectories that the robot could physically execute.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 08 | RECONSTRUCTION -->

  <section class="portfolio-wide-visual">

    <figure>

      <img
        src="/images/projects/robot-milling/bezier-reconstruction.jpg"
        alt="Geometric reconstruction of a Bézier surface"
      />

      <figcaption>
        Surface reconstruction | From geometric data to machinable Bézier geometry
      </figcaption>

    </figure>

  </section>


  <!-- 09 | GEOMETRIC RECONSTRUCTION -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      09
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Geometric reconstruction
      </h2>

      <p>
        A dedicated part of the project focused on reconstructing the target
        surface from available geometric information.
      </p>

      <p>
        The objective was to obtain a mathematical representation sufficiently
        accurate and continuous to support subsequent machining operations.
      </p>

      <p>
        This created a direct link between measurement, geometry reconstruction
        and manufacturing.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 10 | ROBOT KINEMATICS -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      10
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Robot kinematics
      </h2>

      <p>
        A six-axis robot offers a large workspace and significant freedom in tool
        orientation.
      </p>

      <p>
        However, the same tool position can often be reached using several robot
        configurations, each with different stiffness and accessibility.
      </p>

      <p>
        Machining trajectory planning therefore had to consider not only the
        toolpath itself, but also how the complete robot moved along that path.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


